In this Supplementary Material, we present the technical details of the functional renormalization group (FRG) methods employed in this work, together with an in-depth analysis of the emergent orders. In particular, we provide a Ginzburg-Landau (GL) analysis to determine the real-space structures of the triple-\(\mathbf{Q}\) bond order and the nematic charge density wave (CDW) phases. In addition, we evaluate the doping dependence of the orbital magnetization in the LCO phase and make direct comparisons with the experimental systems FeGe and AV\({}_{3}\)Sb\({}_{5}\). The realization of LCO in a spinful model with Zeeman splitting is discussed as well.

## Appendix A Sublattice texture at van Hove filling

On the kagome lattice (Fig. S1(a)), a simple tight binding Hamiltonian with nearest neighbour hybridisation features a bandstructure consisting of a flat band and two dispersive bands crossing at the Dirac point while hosting two different kinds of van-Hove singularities (vHs) displayed in Fig. S1(c). The two vHs are characterized by distinct sublattice texture on the Fermi surface, setting them apart from those observed in triangular and honeycomb lattices. Within this work we focus on the p-type vHs, where each inequivalent M-point consists of states solely attributed to one sublattice (Fig. S1(b)). Correspondingly, the nesting vectors \(\mathbf{Q}_{A,B,C}\) connect saddle points with distinct sublattice characters.

## Appendix B FRG formalism and numerical details

### FRG flow equations

The functional renormalization group (FRG) is an unbiased method to determine Fermi liquid instabilty of interacting fermionic systems [1; 2; 3]. The basic idea of FRG is to introduce a scale dependence of the effective action or generating functional of the one particle irreducible (1PI) vertex functions, \(\Gamma\rightarrow\Gamma^{\Lambda}\). Differentiating \(\Gamma^{\Lambda}\) with respect to \(\Lambda\) leads to exact functional flow equations from which one can interpolate between a microscopic bare action at \(\Lambda\rightarrow\infty\) and a low energy effective action at small \(\Lambda\). Expanding in powers of the fields one obtains an exact hierarchy of flow equations for 1PI vertex functions. We adopt the standard approximations which truncate the hierarchy after two-particle vertex function and discard self-energy feedback, which is appropriate in the weak to intermediate coupling regime. For multi-orbital spinless fermionic systems, the two particle part of the effective action can be expressed by effective interaction \(V^{\Lambda}\) and Grassmann fields \(\psi\) and \(\bar{\psi}\) as

\[\Gamma^{(4)\Lambda}[\bar{\psi},\psi]=\tfrac{1}{(2)^{2}}\int\prod_{i=1}^{4} \mathrm{d}\xi_{i}V^{\Lambda}_{\alpha_{1}\alpha_{2}\alpha_{3}\alpha_{4}}(k_{1}, k_{2};k_{3},k_{4})\delta(k_{1}+k_{2}-k_{3}-k_{4})\bar{\psi}(\xi_{1})\bar{\psi}( \xi_{2})\psi(\xi_{4})\psi(\xi_{3})\,,\] (S1)

where \(k_{i}=(\omega_{i},\mathbf{k}_{i})\) and \(\xi_{i}=(\omega_{i},\mathbf{k}_{i},o_{i})\) are multi-indices gathering a Matsubara frequency \(\omega_{i}\), wave vector \(\mathbf{k}_{i}\), and orbital/sublattice index \(o_{i}\) and \(\mathrm{d}\xi_{i}\) stands for \(\int\frac{d\mathbf{k}_{i}}{S_{BZ}}\frac{1}{\beta}\sum\omega_{i}\sum o_{i}\) with the Brillouin zone area \(S_{BZ}\) and inverse temperature \(\beta\). With this, one can obtain flow equations for the effective two particle interactions

\[\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\Lambda}V^{\Lambda}_{\{o_{i}\}}(k_{1},k_{2};k_{3},k_{4})=\mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{pp}}_{\{o_{i}\}}\left(k_{1},k_{2};k_{3},k_{4} \right)\ \ +\mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{chb}}_{\{o_{i}\}}\left(k_{1},k_{2};k_{3},k_{4} \right)+\mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{chb}}_{\{o_{i}\}}\left(k_{1},k_{2};k_{3},k_{4} \right)\,.\] (S2)The three terms on the right hand side of the flow equation are

\[\mathcal{T}_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{pp}}\left(k_{1},k_{2};k_{3},k_{4}\right) =-\tfrac{1}{2}\int_{q}V_{o_{1}o_{2}oo^{\prime}}^{\Lambda}\left(k_{1 },k_{2};k_{1}+k_{2}+q,-q\right)\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\left[G_{o \dot{\phi}}^{\Lambda}(k_{1}+k_{2}-q)G_{o^{\prime}\dot{\phi}^{\prime}}^{\Lambda} (-q)\right]V_{\partial\partial^{\prime}o_{3}o_{4}}^{\Lambda}\left(k_{1}+k_{2}+q,-q;k_{3},k_{4}\right),\] (S3) \[\mathcal{T}_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{cph}}\left(k_{1},k_{2};k_{3},k_{4}\right) =-\int_{q}V_{o_{1}o^{\prime}oo_{4}}^{\Lambda}\left(k_{1},q;k_{1}- k_{4}+q,k_{4}\right)\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\left[G_{o\dot{\phi}}^{ \Lambda}(k_{1}-k_{4}+q)G_{\phi^{\prime}\sigma^{\prime}}^{\Lambda}(q)\right]V _{\partial o_{2}o_{3}\dot{\phi}^{\prime}}^{\Lambda}\left(k_{1}-k_{4}+q,k_{2};k _{3},q\right),\] \[\mathcal{T}_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{dph}}\left(k_{1},k_{2};k_{3},k_{4} \right) =+\int_{q}V_{o_{1}o^{\prime}o_{3}o}^{\Lambda}\left(k_{1},q;k_{3},k _{1}-k_{3}+q\right)\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\left[G_{o\dot{\phi}}^ {\Lambda}(k_{1}-k_{3}+q)G_{\phi^{\prime}\sigma^{\prime}}^{\Lambda}(q)\right]V _{\partial o_{2}\dot{\phi}^{\prime}o_{4}}^{\Lambda}\left(k_{1}-k_{3}+q,k_{2};q,k_{4}\right)\,\] (S4)

which represent contributions to the interactions in particle-particle, cross particle-hole, and direct particle-hole channels, respectively, with the diagrammatic representations shown in Fig. S2. Here \(G_{oo^{\prime}}^{\Lambda}\) is the bare propagator in orbital basis at scale \(\Lambda\) and \(\int_{q}=\sum_{o\sigma^{\prime}\bar{o}\bar{o}^{\prime}}\tfrac{1}{\beta}\sum_{ \omega}\int\tfrac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{q}}{S_{\text{B}\alpha}}\). We will further ignore the frequency dependence of effective interaction since interactions at finite frequencies are irrelevant under the renormalization group transformations and we focus on static ground-state properties.

### Truncated unity FRG

In the singular mode or truncated unity functional renormalization group (TUFRG) formalism [4; 5], the static four point vertex function is decomposed into a scale independent initial bare interaction \(V^{(0)}\) plus three coupling functions \(\Phi^{X},X\in\{P,C,D\}\),

\[V_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\Lambda}(\mathbf{k}_{1},\mathbf{k}_{2};\mathbf{k} _{3},\mathbf{k}_{4}) =V_{\{o_{i}\}}^{(0)}(\mathbf{k}_{1},\mathbf{k}_{2};\mathbf{k}_{3}, \mathbf{k}_{4})+\Phi_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{P}}\left(\mathbf{k}_{1}+\mathbf{k}_{2},-\mathbf{k}_{2},-\mathbf{k}_{4}\right)\] (S5) \[+\Phi_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{C}}\left(\mathbf{k}_{1}-\mathbf{k}_{4}, \mathbf{k}_{4},-\mathbf{k}_{2}\right)+\Phi_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{D}}\left(\mathbf{ k}_{1}-\mathbf{k}_{3},\mathbf{k}_{3},-\mathbf{k}_{2}\right)\,,\]

which are generated during the flows according to

\[\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\Phi_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{P}} \left(\mathbf{k}_{1}+\mathbf{k}_{2},-\mathbf{k}_{2},-\mathbf{k}_{4}\right)= \mathcal{T}_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{pp}}\left(\mathbf{k}_{1},\mathbf{k}_{2};\mathbf{ k}_{3},\mathbf{k}_{4}\right)\] (S6) \[\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\Phi_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{D}} \left(\mathbf{k}_{1}-\mathbf{k}_{4},\mathbf{k}_{4},-\mathbf{k}_{2}\right)= \mathcal{T}_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{cph}}\left(\mathbf{k}_{1},\mathbf{k}_{2}; \mathbf{k}_{3},\mathbf{k}_{4}\right)\] \[\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\lambda}\Phi_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{D}} \left(\mathbf{k}_{1}-\mathbf{k}_{3},\mathbf{k}_{3},-\mathbf{k}_{2}\right)= \mathcal{T}_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{dph}}\left(\mathbf{k}_{1},\mathbf{k}_{2}; \mathbf{k}_{3},\mathbf{k}_{4}\right)\,.\]

The first argument of \(\Phi^{X}\) is transfer momentum and the last two are non-transfer momenta. We then expand the less important non-transfer momenta into a formfactor basis \(\{f_{m}(\mathbf{k})\}\),

\[\Phi_{\{o_{i}\}}^{\text{X}}\left(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime} \right)=\sum_{m,n}f_{m}^{*}(\mathbf{k})X_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4},mn}(\mathbf{q})f_ {n}(\mathbf{k}^{\prime})\] (S7)

