### Regulators

The scale dependence of the bare propagator \(G_{oo^{\prime}}\) is facilitated by a cutoff function \(\Theta^{\Lambda}\) (regulator) according to \(G^{\Lambda}_{\sigma\sigma^{\prime}}=\Theta^{\Lambda}G_{oo^{\prime}}\)[3; 6]. The choice of regulators is limited by the behavior in its limits of \(\Lambda\) i.e. \(\Phi^{\Lambda\to\infty}=0\) and \(\Phi^{\Lambda\to 0}=1\). The approximations applied to numerically evaluate the flow equations introduce a regulator dependence, even though the general scheme of FRG is independent on the choice of regulators. Within this work, we observed qualitative agreement of \(\Omega\)-cutoff, sharp-frequency-cutoff [3; 6] and temperature cutoff [7]. The results displayed in the main part and supplemental material are obtained by FRG calculations utilizing the \(\Omega\)-cutoff. An exception from this is Fig. S6, here the temperature cutoff [7] is applied. In the temperature flow scheme, the fermionic fields are rescaled such that only the quadratic part of the action is temperature dependent. This leads to a temperature-scaled free propagator and the temperature is treated as flow parameters, i.e.

\[G_{0}(i\omega,\mathbf{k})\to G_{0}^{T}(i\omega,\mathbf{k})=\tfrac{T^{1/2}}{i \omega-\xi(\mathbf{k})}\] (S15)

and the particle-particle(-) and particle-hole(+) bubbles are

\[\begin{split}\dot{\chi}^{\Lambda}_{\pm}(\mathbf{k_{1}},\mathbf{k_ {2}})&=\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}T}\sum_{i\omega_{n}}G^{T}( \mathbf{k_{1}},i\omega_{n})G^{T}(\mathbf{k_{2}},\pm i\omega_{n})=\tfrac{ \mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}T}\sum_{i\omega_{n}}T\tfrac{1}{i\omega_{n}-\xi_{\mathbf{ k_{1}}}}\tfrac{1}{\pm i\omega_{n}-\xi_{\mathbf{k_{2}}}}\\ &=\tfrac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}T}\tfrac{n_{F}(\pm\xi_{\mathbf{k_{1} }})-n_{F}(\xi_{\mathbf{k_{2}}})}{\xi_{\mathbf{k_{1}}}\mp\xi_{\mathbf{k_{2}}}}= \tfrac{n_{F}^{{}^{\prime}}(\pm\xi_{\mathbf{k_{1}}})-n_{F}^{{}^{\prime}}(\xi_{ \mathbf{k_{2}}})}{\xi_{\mathbf{k_{1}}}\mp\xi_{\mathbf{k_{2}}}}\,,\end{split}\] (S16)

where \(n_{F}^{{}^{\prime}}\) stands for temperature derivative Fermi function.

### Initial conditions

Due to the antisymmetry of Grassman fields, the spinless two particle vertex is antisymmetric with respect to exchange of two incoming or outgoing particles

\[V_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4}}(\mathbf{k_{1}},\mathbf{k_{2}};\mathbf{k_{3}},\mathbf{ k_{4}})=-V_{o_{2}o_{1}o_{3}o_{4}}(\mathbf{k_{2}},\mathbf{k_{1}};\mathbf{k_{3}}, \mathbf{k_{4}}),V_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4}}(\mathbf{k_{1}},\mathbf{k_{2}};\mathbf{ k_{3}},\mathbf{k_{4}})=-V_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{4}o_{3}}(\mathbf{k_{1}},\mathbf{k_{2}}; \mathbf{k_{4}},\mathbf{k_{3}})\,.\] (S17)

To obtain initial conditions for the FRG flow, the bare interaction terms need to be antisymmetrized properly,

\[\begin{split}& V_{n}\sum_{\mathbf{R}_{i},\mathbf{R}_{n}}n_{o}( \mathbf{R}_{i})n_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{n})=V_{n}\sum_{\mathbf{ R}_{i},\mathbf{R}_{n}}c^{\dagger}_{o}(\mathbf{R}_{i})c^{\dagger}_{o^{\prime}}( \mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{n})c_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{n})c_{o} (\mathbf{R}_{i})\\ &=\tfrac{1}{4}V_{n}\sum_{\mathbf{k_{1}},\mathbf{k_{2}},\mathbf{k_ {3}}}\Big{(}e^{i(\mathbf{k_{1}}-\mathbf{k_{3}})\cdot\mathbf{R}_{n}}c^{\dagger}_{o }(\mathbf{k_{1}})c^{\dagger}_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{k_{2}})c_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{ k_{4}})c_{o}(\mathbf{k_{3}})-e^{i(\mathbf{k_{2}}-\mathbf{k_{3}})\cdot\mathbf{R}_{n}}c^{ \dagger}_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{k}_{1})c^{\dagger}_{o}(\mathbf{k_{2}})c_{o^{\prime}}( \mathbf{k}_{4})c_{o}(\mathbf{k_{3}})\\ &\quad\quad-e^{i(\mathbf{k_{1}}-\mathbf{k_{4}})\cdot\mathbf{R}_{n}}c^{ \dagger}_{o}(\mathbf{k_{1}})c^{\dagger}_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{k_{2}})c_{o}( \mathbf{k_{4}})c_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{k_{3}})+e^{i(\mathbf{k_{2}}-\mathbf{k_{4}}) \cdot\mathbf{R}_{n}})c^{\prime}_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{k_{1}})c^{\dagger}_{o}( \mathbf{k_{2}})c_{o}(\mathbf{k_{4}})c_{o^{\prime}}(\mathbf{k_{3}})\,\end{split}\] (S18)where Bravais lattice vectors \(\mathbf{R}_{n}\) are shown Fig. 10(b). Then vertex functions are obtained from the above equation and inserted into Eq. (S10) to determine valid initial conditions. For an inter-sublattice density-density interaction with \(o\neq o^{\prime}\), the initial interactions in \(P\), \(C\) and \(D\) channel are given by

\[\begin{split} V^{\mathrm{P,0}}_{o\sigma^{\prime}oo^{\prime},- \mathbf{R}_{n},-\mathbf{R}_{n}}(\mathbf{q})&=V_{n},\ V^{P,0}_{o^{ \prime}ooo^{\prime},\mathbf{R}_{n},-\mathbf{R}_{n}}(\mathbf{q})=-V_{n}e^{-i \mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{R}_{n}},\ V^{\mathrm{P,0}}_{o\sigma^{\prime}o,-\mathbf{ R}_{n},\mathbf{R}_{n}}(\mathbf{q})=-V_{n}e^{i\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{R}_{n}},\ V^{\mathrm{P,0}}_{o^{\prime}oo^{\prime},\mathbf{R}_{n}, \mathbf{R}_{n}}(\mathbf{q})=V_{n},\\ V^{\mathrm{C,0}}_{o\sigma o^{\prime},-\mathbf{R}_{n},-\mathbf{R}_{ n}}(\mathbf{q})&=V_{n},\ V^{C,0}_{o\sigma o^{\prime},\mathbf{R}_{1},\mathbf{R}_{1}}( \mathbf{q})=-V_{n}\sum_{\mathbf{R}_{n}}e^{-i\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{R}_{n}},\ V^{C,0}_{o\sigma^{\prime}o, \mathbf{R}_{1},\mathbf{R}_{1}}(\mathbf{q})=-V_{n}\sum_{\mathbf{R}_{n}}e^{i \mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{R}_{n}},\ V^{C,0}_{o^{\prime}oo^{\prime}o,\mathbf{R}_{n},\mathbf{R}_{n}}(\mathbf{q})=V_{n},\\ V^{\mathrm{D,0}}_{o\sigma o^{\prime}o,\mathbf{R}_{1},\mathbf{R}_{1}}( \mathbf{q})&=V_{n}\sum_{\mathbf{R}_{n}}e^{i\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{ R}_{n}},\ V^{\mathrm{D,0}}_{o^{\prime}ooo^{\prime},\mathbf{R}_{n},\mathbf{R}_{n}}(\mathbf{q})=-V_{n},\ V^{\mathrm{D,0}}_{o^{\prime}oo,-\mathbf{R}_{n},-\mathbf{R}_{n}}( \mathbf{q})=-V_{n},\ V^{\mathrm{D,0}}_{o^{\prime}oo^{\prime}o,\mathbf{R}_{1}, \mathbf{R}_{1}}(\mathbf{q})=V_{n}\sum_{\mathbf{R}_{n}}e^{-i\mathbf{q}\cdot \mathbf{R}_{n}}.\end{split}\] (S19)

### Instability analysis

The FRG flow equations can be integrated to get the renormalized effective interaction by reducing the FRG scale \(\Lambda\). The flow will be terminated at scale \(\Lambda_{c}\) upon encountering a divergence in a channel, which indicates the normal metallic phase may become unstable towards a symmetry-broken state. The critical scale \(\Lambda_{c}\) provides an estimate for the critical Temperature \(T_{c}\). The divergent vertex component signals the type of symmetry-broken phase the system might enter. Projecting effective interactions of the employed spinless model into superconducting and charge channels, the effective interaction for superconductivity (SC) and CDW orders are

\[\begin{split}\hat{V}^{\mathrm{SC}}&=\sum_{\mathbf{q },\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime},\{o_{i}\}}V^{\mathrm{P}}_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4}} \left(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime}\right)c^{\dagger}_{\mathbf{k}+ \mathbf{q},o_{1}}c^{\dagger}_{-\mathbf{k},o_{2}}c_{-\mathbf{k}^{\prime},o_{4}} c_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime}+\mathbf{q},o_{3}}\\ \hat{V}^{\mathrm{CDW}}&=\sum_{\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k },\mathbf{k}^{\prime},\{o_{i}\}}V^{\mathrm{D}}_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4}}\left( \mathbf{q},\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime}\right)c^{\dagger}_{\mathbf{k}+ \mathbf{q},o_{1}}c_{\mathbf{k},o_{3}}c^{\dagger}_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime},o_{2}} c_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime}+\mathbf{q},o_{4}}\end{split}\] (S20)

with

\[\hat{V}^{\mathrm{X}}_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4}}\left(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k},\mathbf{ k}^{\prime}\right)=\sum_{m,n}f^{*}_{m}(\mathbf{k})V^{\mathrm{X}}_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4},mn}( \mathbf{q})f_{n}(\mathbf{k}^{\prime})\,.\] (S21)

With plane wave formfactors, plugging Eq. (S20) into Eq. (S21) leads to effective interactions in real space

\[\begin{split}\hat{V}^{\mathrm{SC}}&=\sum_{\mathbf{q },\mathbf{R}_{i},\mathbf{R}_{j},m,n,\{o_{i}\}}V^{\mathrm{P}}_{o_{1}o_{2}m,o_{3} o_{4}n}\left(\mathbf{q}\right)e^{i\mathbf{q}\cdot\left(\mathbf{R}_{i}+ \mathbf{R}_{m}\right)}c^{\dagger}_{o_{1}}(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{m})c^{ \dagger}_{o_{2}}(\mathbf{R}_{i})c_{o_{4}}(\mathbf{R}_{j})c_{o_{3}}(\mathbf{ R}_{j}+\mathbf{R}_{n})e^{-i\mathbf{q}\cdot\left(\mathbf{R}_{j}+\mathbf{R}_{n} \right)}\\ \hat{V}^{\mathrm{CDW}}&=\sum_{\mathbf{q},\mathbf{R} _{i},\mathbf{R}_{j},m,n\{o_{i}\}}V^{\mathrm{D}}_{o_{1}o_{3}m,o_{4}o_{2}n} \left(\mathbf{q}\right)e^{i\mathbf{q}\cdot\left(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{m} \right)}c^{\dagger}_{o_{1}}(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{m})c_{o_{3}}(\mathbf{ R}_{i})c^{\dagger}_{o_{2}}(\mathbf{R}_{j})c_{o_{4}}(\mathbf{R}_{j}+\mathbf{R}_{n})e^{-i \mathbf{q}\cdot\left(\mathbf{R}_{j}+\mathbf{R}_{n}\right)}.\end{split}\] (S22)

Defining order parameter in momentum space

\[\begin{split}\Delta^{\mathrm{SC,\mathbf{q}}}_{o_{1}o_{2}}(\mathbf{k} )&=-\sum_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime},o_{4},o_{4}}V^{\mathrm{P}}_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4}} \left(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime}\right)\left\langle c_{-\mathbf{ k}^{\prime},o_{4}}c_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime}+\mathbf{q},o_{3}}\right\rangle,\\ \Delta^{\mathrm{CDW,\mathbf{q}}}_{o_{1}o_{2}}(\mathbf{k})& =-\sum_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime},o_{4},o_{4}}V^{\mathrm{D}}_{o_{1}o_{2}o_{3}o_{4}} \left(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime}\right)\left\langle c^{\dagger}_{ \mathbf{k}^{\prime},o_{4}}c_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime}+\mathbf{q},o_{3}}\right\rangle, \end{split}\] (S23)

the above effective interaction can be mean field decoupled, which leads to linearized gap equations

\[\begin{split}&-\sum_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime},o^{\prime}_{1}o^{\prime}_{2},o^{ \prime}_{3}o^{\prime}_{4}}V^{\mathrm{P}}_{o_{1}o_{2},o_{3}o_{4}}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime})L^{\mathrm{pp}}_{o_{3}o_{4},o^{\prime}_{1}o^{ \prime}_{2}}(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k}^{\prime})\Delta^{\mathrm{SC,\mathbf{q}}}_{o^{ \prime}o_{2}}(\mathbf{k}^{\prime})=\lambda\Delta^{\mathrm{SC,\mathbf{q}}}_{o_{1}o_{2}} (\mathbf{k}).\\ &-\sum_{\mathbf{k}^{\prime},o^{\prime}_{1}o^{\prime}_{2},o^{\prime}_{3} o^{\prime}_{4}}V^{\mathrm{D}}_{o_{1}o_{2},o_{3}o_{4}}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime})L^{\mathrm{ph}}_{o_{3}o_{4},o^{\prime}_{1}o^{ \prime}_{2}}(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k}^{\prime})\Delta^{\mathrm{CDW,\mathbf{q}}}_{o^{ \prime}_{1}o^{\prime}_{2}}(\mathbf{k}^{\prime})=\lambda\Delta^{\mathrm{CDW, \mathbf{q}}}_{o_{1}o_{2}}(\mathbf{k}).\end{split}\] (S24)

In formfactor basis, this equation has the following matrix multiplication form

\[\begin{split}&-\sum_{o^{\prime}_{1}o^{\prime}_{2}m^{\prime},o^{ \prime}_{3}o^{\prime}_{4}n^{\prime}}V^{\mathrm{P}}_{o_{1}o_{2}m,o_{3}o_{4}m^{ \prime}}(\mathbf{q})Here we define the real space order parameter

\[\begin{split}\Delta^{\text{SC},\mathbf{q}}_{o_{1}o_{2}m}=\int\text{ d}\mathbf{k}f_{m}(\mathbf{k})\Delta^{\text{SC},\mathbf{q}}_{o_{1}o_{2}}(\mathbf{k})=- \sum_{j,n,o_{4},o_{4}}V^{\text{P}}_{o_{1}o_{2}m,o_{3}o_{4}n}\left(\mathbf{q} \right)\left\langle c_{o_{4}}(\mathbf{R}_{j})c_{o_{3}}(\mathbf{R}_{j}+\mathbf{ R}_{n})\right\rangle e^{-i\mathbf{q}\cdot(\mathbf{R}_{j}+\mathbf{R}_{n})}\\ \Delta^{\text{CDW},\mathbf{q}}_{o_{1}o_{2}m}=\int\text{d}\mathbf{ k}f_{m}(\mathbf{k})\Delta^{\text{CDW},\mathbf{q}}_{o_{1}o_{2}}(\mathbf{k})=- \sum_{j,n,o_{4},o_{4}}V^{\text{D}}_{o_{1}o_{2}m,o_{3}o_{4}n}\left(\mathbf{q} \right)\left\langle c^{\dagger}_{o_{4}}(\mathbf{R}_{j})c_{o_{3}}(\mathbf{R}_{ j}+\mathbf{R}_{n})\right\rangle e^{-i\mathbf{q}\cdot(\mathbf{R}_{j}+\mathbf{R}_{n})}. \end{split}\] (S26)

Then the effective interaction in real space can be mean-field decoupled to yield

\[\begin{split} V^{\text{SC}}&=-\sum_{\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{R}_{i},m,o_{1},o_{2}}\Delta^{\text{SC},\mathbf{q}}_{o_{1}o_{2},m}e^{i \mathbf{q}\cdot(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{m})}c^{\dagger}_{o_{1}}(\mathbf{R} _{i}+\mathbf{R}_{m})c^{\dagger}_{o_{2}}(\mathbf{R}_{i})+h.c.\\ V^{\text{CDW}}&=-\sum_{\mathbf{q},\mathbf{R}_{i},m, o_{1},o_{2}}\Delta^{\text{CDW},\mathbf{q}}_{o_{1}o_{2},m}e^{i\mathbf{q} \cdot(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{m})}c^{\dagger}_{o_{1}}(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{ R}_{m})c_{o_{2}}(\mathbf{R}_{i})+h.c.\end{split}\] (S27)

omitting constant terms. Then by solving the linearized gap equation Eq. (S25), we obtain the real space pairing pattern \(\Delta^{\text{SC},\mathbf{q}}_{o_{1}o_{2}m}\) and \(\Delta^{\text{CDW},\mathbf{q}}_{o_{1}o_{2},m}\), which describes the SC and CDW paring on bond \(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{m}\) and \(\mathbf{R}_{i}\) modulated by a phase \(e^{i\mathbf{q}\cdot(\mathbf{R}_{i}+\mathbf{R}_{m})}\).

From effective interactions, we can also analyze leading order by diagonlizing interaction corrected renormalized susceptibility in particle-hole and particle-particle channels

\[\begin{split}\chi^{\text{PDW,I}}(\mathbf{q})&=\chi ^{\text{pp}}(\mathbf{q})\left(-V^{\text{P}}(\mathbf{q})\right)\chi^{\text{pp }}(\mathbf{q}),\\ \chi^{\text{CDW,I}}(\mathbf{q})&=\chi^{\text{ph}}( \mathbf{q})\left(-V^{\text{D}}(\mathbf{q})\right)\chi^{\text{ph}}(\mathbf{q}), \end{split}\] (S28)

from which we obtain the components of bond and cuurent order in the main text.

### Identifying CBO and LCO parameters from vertex eigenstates

As discussed in the previous section, the outcome of an FRG flow is the low energy effective vertex in the three different fluctuation channels. The order parameter manifesting at a divergence of the FRG susceptibilities is hence obtained by solving the linearised gap equation in the respective channel. This boils down to the diagonalisation of the effective vertex at each transfer momentum \(\mathbf{q}\), where the eigenstate corresponding to the largest eigenvalue gives the ordering vector of the symmetry broken phase. Since the overall phase of the eigenstates is gauge dependent, this poses the question how to distinguish between real and imaginary bond orders.

On the Kagome lattice the FRG vertex diverges at the three \(\mathbf{M}\) points at the edges of the BZ. While this usually requires a subsequent Ginzburg-Landau analysis to fix the relative phases between the degenerate states at the \(\mathbf{M}\) points [8], the formfactor information encoded in the eigenstates is already sufficient to distinguish CBO from LCO states by means of a single \(\mathbf{M}\) point instability. As discussed in the main text, the instability at each \(\mathbf{M}\) point corresponds to a real space order on perpendicular bonds. As \(\mathbf{M}=-\mathbf{M}\), this implies, that the order parameter on one specific bond is uniquely determined by the eigenstate of a single \(\mathbf{M}\) point (compare Fig. S5). Furthermore, the knowledge of both \(\langle c^{\dagger}_{i}c_{j}\rangle\) and \(\langle c^{\dagger}_{j}c_{i}\rangle\) and its relative phase as provided by the formfactor indices of the FRG order parameter, allows for a direct classification as real or imaginary bond order

\[\begin{split}\Delta^{\text{CBO}}_{ij}=\langle c^{\dagger}_{i}c_{j }+c^{\dagger}_{j}c_{i}\rangle=\langle c^{\dagger}_{i}c_{j}\rangle+\langle c^{ \dagger}_{j}c_{i}\rangle\\ \Delta^{\text{LCO}}_{ij}=\text{i}\langle c^{\dagger}_{i}c_{j}-c^ {\dagger}_{j}c_{i}\rangle=\text{i}\langle(c^{\dagger}_{i}c_{j})-\langle c^{ \dagger}_{j}c_{i}\rangle\rangle\,\end{split}\] (S29)since the global gauge is fixed by the requirement for an hermitian order parameter.

Appendix C An intuitive picture of the competing real and imaginary bond orders on the kagome lattice

As discussed in the main text, the bond orders on the kagome lattice emerge from the VHS nesting at the Fermi level and can be characterised by a \(\mathbf{M}\) point periodicity. On the kagome lattice, this reflects in a sign change of the order parameter between neighbouring 1D chains along the \(\mathbf{M}\) point direction, while the bond order establishes along the respective chain (see Fig. S5). Let us study the \(V_{2}=0\) limit first, _i.e._ consider only nearest neighbor bond orders. In this case, it is possible to discuss the emergence of bond order instabilities by considering the 1D chains independently, since both the hopping and interaction terms only couple states within one single chain.

### Mean field energy gap

Bond orders in both real and imaginary channel are three fold degenerate with ordering wave vectors \(\mathbf{Q}_{A,B,C}\). We exemplarily consider a bond order at \(\mathbf{Q}_{A}\) in the following. The effective mean field Hamiltonian in momentum space reads

\[H_{\text{LCO}}=\sum_{\mathbf{k}}\Delta(f_{\mathbf{k}}c_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_ {C},A}^{\dagger}c_{\mathbf{k},B}+f_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{C}}c_{\mathbf{k},A }^{\dagger}c_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{C},B}+f_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{A}}c_{ \mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{B},A}^{\dagger}c_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{A},A}c_{ \mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{B},B})+\text{H.c.}\,,\] (S30)

where \(\Delta\) is the order parameter and \(f_{\mathbf{k}}\) is the corresponding formfactor. Then the effective Hamiltonian describing the scattering between two Fermi points \(\mathbf{k}=(-\pi,k_{y})\) and \(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{C}=(\pi,k_{y})\) reads

\[h=(\Delta f_{\mathbf{k}}u_{A}^{*}(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{C})u_{B}(\mathbf{k})+ \Delta^{*}f_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{C}}^{*}u_{B}^{*}(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{C })u_{A}(\mathbf{k}))c_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{C}}^{\dagger}c_{\mathbf{k}}+ \text{H.c.}\,.\] (S31)

At the Van-Hove point, \(k_{y}=-\pi/\sqrt{3}\) and \(u_{A}(\mathbf{k})=u_{B}(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{c})=1,u_{B}(\mathbf{k})=u_{A}( \mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{c})=0\) and the gap opening at the Fermi level is given by \(\Delta_{\text{VH}}=|\Delta f_{\mathbf{M}_{A}}|\). The form factor reads \(f_{\mathbf{k}}=\cos(\mathbf{k}\cdot\delta)\) for the symmetrized bond order and \(f_{\mathbf{k}}=i\sin(\mathbf{k}\cdot\delta)\) for anti-symmetrized bond order.

As \(\mathbf{M}_{A}\cdot\delta=\pi/2\) for \(\delta=(1/2,0)\) or \((0,\sqrt{3}/2)\), symmetrized bond orders cannot gap out the Van-Hove points and are hence energetically unfavorable. At the center between two Van-Hove points, \(k_{y}=0\) and \(u_{A}(\mathbf{k})=u_{B}(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{c})=u_{B}(\mathbf{k})=u_{A}( \mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{c})=1/\sqrt{2}\). Considering anti-symmetric formfactors \(f_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{Q}_{C}}^{*}=\pm f_{\mathbf{k}}\) for \(\delta=(1/2,0)\) and \((0,\sqrt{3}/2)\), the gap opening of an anti-symmetrized bond order at the middle point \(\Delta_{\text{Mid}}=|\Delta\pm\Delta^{*}||f_{(-\pi,0)}|/2\). Hence the bond order will open a lager gap in real channel on 1NN bond and imaginary channel on 2NN bond around the middle point of Van-Hove points as shown in Fig. S4.

### Nearest neighbour bond orders

The four possible nearest neighbour bond orders on the 1D chain can be cathegorized by their transformation behaviour under inversion and time reversal symmetry (TRS) into the real symmetric (even) and antisymmetric (odd) bond orders

\[\ket{\text{CBO}}_{e}= \sum_{i}(c_{2i+1}^{\dagger}c_{2i}+c_{2i+2}^{\dagger}c_{2i+1}+h.c.) \ket{\Phi_{0}}\] (S32) \[\ket{\text{CBO}}_{o}= \sum_{i}(c_{2i+1}^{\dagger}c_{2i}-c_{2i+2}^{\dagger}c_{2i+1}+h.c.) \ket{\Phi_{0}}\]

and the loop current orders

\[\ket{\text{LCO}}_{e}= \sum_{i}(c_{2i+1}^{\dagger}c_{2i}+c_{2i+2}^{\dagger}c_{2i+1}-h.c.) \ket{\Phi_{0}}\] (S33) \[\ket{\text{LCO}}_{o}= \sum_{i}(c_{2i+1}^{\dagger}c_{2i}-c_{2i+2}^{\dagger}c_{2i+1}-h.c.) \ket{\Phi_{0}}\ \,\]

where \(i\) labels the site position and the operators act on the ground state of the non-interacting Hamiltonian \(\ket{\Phi_{0}}=\rho\sum_{i}c_{i}^{\dagger}c_{i}\ket{0}\). The electron density per site \(\rho=5/12\) at p-type vH filling. Inspecting the real space structure of the orders, as depicted in Fig. S5, already two of these orders can be discarded from physical arguments: Interpreting the CBO as a generalised Peierls instability, _i.e._ the increased hybridisation strength between two sites originates from a shift in the Wannier centers towards the bond center, the symmetric bond order is not volume preserving and hence not compatible with Born-von Karman periodic boundary conditions. The asymmetric LCO on the other hand, leads to charge accumulation on every second sites and can therefore not be realized in thermodynamic equilibrium. Indeed both of these ordering tendencies show little susceptibility in our numerical calculations [9]. In the main text as well as in the following, we therefore denote with CBO and LCO only the physically meaningful instabilities \(\ket{\text{CBO}}=\ket{\text{CBO}}_{o}\) and \(\ket{\text{LCO}}=\ket{\text{LCO}}_{e}\).

### Ginzburg-Landau analysis of real and imaginary bond orders

To pinpoint the origin of the surprising correlation between the NNN interaction and the emergence of LCO, we expand the free energy in the symmetry broken phase up to second order in the bond order parameter \(\Delta_{i}\) to obtain

\[f^{(2)}=\tfrac{1}{g}\sum_{i}|\Delta_{i}|^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i}\text{Tr}((G \Delta_{i})^{2})\] (S34)

with the bare single-particle Greensfunction \(G\) and some renormalized interaction strength \(g\). \(\Delta_{i}\) is the bond order modulation corresponding to the eigenstate of the vertex at \(M_{i}\). While the first term is equal for real and imaginary bond orders, the second term can lead to an energy difference between the CBO and LCO states.

As discussed earlier, the order parameter associated with a single \(M_{i}\) instability uniquely determines the bond order profile along the corresponding real space direction (cf. Fig. S5) and does not couple to the other direction up to second order in the GL functional. Hence, let us for simplicity consider a single \(\mathbf{M}\) order and evaluate the second term in Eq. (S34) for the two competing NN orders

\[\Delta^{\text{CBO}}= \sum_{i}\langle(c_{2i+1}^{\dagger}c_{2i}-c_{2i+2}^{\dagger}c_{2i+ 1})+h.c.\rangle\] (S35) \[\Delta^{\text{LCO}}= \sum_{i}\langle i(c_{2i+1}^{\dagger}c_{2i}+c_{2i+2}^{\dagger}c_{2 i+1})+h.c.\rangle\.\]

Here, \(i\) labels the real space positions along the 1D chain. It is instructive to do this in real space to obtain

\[\text{Tr}(G\Delta G\Delta)\sim \sum_{i}G_{i,i}\Delta_{i,i+1}G_{i+1,i+1}\Delta_{i+1,i}+G_{i,i} \Delta_{i,i-1}G_{i-1,i-1}\Delta_{i-1,i}\] (S36) \[+G_{i,i+1}\Delta_{i+1,i+2}G_{i+2,i+1}G_{i+1,i}+G_{i,i-1}\Delta_{i -1,i-2}G_{i-2,i-1}\Delta_{i-1,i}\,\]

where we only take into account short range scattering terms up to first nearest neighbour. The energy difference between the imaginary and real bond order is then easily traced back to its distinct transformation behaviour under translation by one lattice site \(\Delta_{i,i+1}\rightarrow\Delta_{i+1,i+2}\). Using the relations

\[\Delta^{\text{LCO}}_{i,i+1}=(\Delta^{\text{LCO}}_{i+1,i})^{*}\,\ \Delta^{\text{LCO}}_{i+1,i+2}= (\Delta^{\text{LCO}}_{i+1,i})^{*}\,\] (S37) \[\Delta^{\text{CBO}}_{i,i+1}=(\Delta^{\text{CBO}}_{i+1,i})^{*}\,\ \Delta^{ \text{CBO}}_{i+1,i+2}= -(\Delta^{\text{CBO}}_{i+1,i})^{*}\,\]

one can distinguish two type of terms: Scattering events involving translations by an even number of sites (corresponding to to equal sublattice indices of the Green functions on the kagome lattice)

\[\Pi=|\Delta|^{2}\sum_{i}G_{i,i}G_{i+1,i+1}+G_{i,i}G_{i-1,i-1}\] (S38)

contribute equally to the free energy of CBO and LCO. Terms with translations by an odd number of sites

\[\Xi=|\Delta|^{2}\sum_{i}G_{i,i+1}G_{i+2,i+1}+G_{i,i-1}G_{i-2,i-1}\] (S39)

are distinct between CBO and LCO, such that we obtain under the made approximations

\[f_{\text{CBO/LCO}}=\Pi\pm\Xi\.\] (S40)

The situation is reversed for the case of second nearest neighbor order. Now the NN hopping events flip the sign of the LCO and the CBO is invariant under a translation by one lattice constant along the 1D chains (cf. Fig. S5). While again the \(\Pi\) term is equivalent for both orders, the sign of \(\Xi\) is reversed and the LCO has a higher energy gain. The analysis of the short ranged scattering events reveals a natural correspondence between the energy gain of the bond orders and their real space structure. The obtained result find solid ground in RPA susceptibility calculations, where the real space information is encoded in the formfactor dependence of the particle-hole bubble [9].

This sheds new light on the necessity of second nearest neighbor interactions in driving LCO in the kagome Hubbard model as already speculated on MF level in Ref. [10]. The LCO can only emerge as a self-sufficient instability due to coupling of the individual 1D kagome chains by second nearest neighbour formfactors. This allows for an energygain by screening processes mediated by a single hopping event. Apparently, this argument is tightly bound to the presence of nearest neighbor hybridisation only. We expect second nearest neighbor hybridisation \(t^{\prime}\) to strengthen the NN LCO compared to the NN CBO as this unlocks additional first order processes for energy gains within the LCO phase. Since the bare interaction couples predominantly to bond order fluctuations of the same length scale, the \(V_{2}/V_{1}\) allows for a tuning between CBO and LCO, where the CBO (LCO) is predominantly found in the \(V_{1}>(<)V_{2}\) regime.

### CBO in the \(V_{1}\) dominated regime

In the \(V_{1}\) dominated regime, we find robust charge bond order compared to the relative fragile LCO of the \(V_{2}\) dominated regime. A typical flow in the CBO region is shown in Fig. S6(a). The obtained charge bond modulations predominantly occur on nearest neighbour (1NN) bonds as shown in Fig. S6(b). This is consistent with RPA results where \(V_{1}\) enhances real bond susceptibilities compared to imaginary ones and favors the formation of CBO [9]. The CBO is three fold degenerate with ordering wave vectors \(\mathbf{Q}_{A}\), \(\mathbf{Q}_{B}\), \(\mathbf{Q}_{C}\), which modulate bonds along \(BC,CA,AB\) directions respectively. A trilinear term \(\Delta_{A}\Delta_{B}\Delta_{C}\) in Ginzburg-Landau free energy will favor the formation of \(3\mathbf{Q}\) other than \(3\mathbf{Q}\) CBO. The effective mean field Hamiltonian is described by

\[H_{\text{CBO}}=-\sum_{\alpha\beta\gamma\mathbf{R}}\epsilon_{\alpha\beta\gamma }\Delta_{\gamma}\left(c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}(\mathbf{R})c_{\beta}(\mathbf{R})+c_ {\alpha}^{\dagger}(\mathbf{R})c_{\beta}(\mathbf{R}-\mathbf{d}_{\beta\gamma}^{ \text{NN}})\right)\cos(\mathbf{Q}_{\gamma}\cdot\mathbf{R})+h.c.\,,\] (S41)

where \(\Delta_{\gamma}\) is the real order parameter. Six fold rotational symmetry is preserved when \(\Delta_{\gamma}=\Delta\) for \(\gamma=A,B,C\). A real \(\Delta>0(\Delta<0)\) will correspond to trihexagonal(anti-trihexagonal) patterns. The trihexagonal pattern is shown in Fig. S6(c).

The subdominant SC fluctuations in CBO phase exhibit \(p\)-wave symmetry. As shown in Fig. S6(d), the corresponding paring functions on FS exhibit nodes not only along \(\Gamma-M\) but also along \(\Gamma-K\) direction. Thus compared with \(B_{1}\)\(f\)-wave SC, the \(E_{2}\)\(p\)-wave SC has lower condensation energy and does not appear as dominant instability in \(V_{1}\) dominated regime.

## Appendix D Physical properties of the LCO state

### Ginzburg-Landau analysis and real space pattern

Within the LCO regime, the low energy effective interaction is dominated by scattering in the LCO channel. Thus the effective effective mean field Hamiltonian can be written as

\[H_{\text{LCO}}=-\sum_{\alpha\beta\gamma\mathbf{R}}\epsilon_{\alpha\beta\gamma }i\Delta_{\gamma}\left(c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}(\mathbf{R}+\mathbf{d}_{\beta\gamma }^{\text{2NN}})c_{\beta}(\mathbf{R})+c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}(\mathbf{R}+\mathbf{ d}_{\beta\gamma}^{\text{2NN}^{\prime}})c_{\beta}(\mathbf{R})\right)\cos( \mathbf{Q}_{\gamma}\cdot\mathbf{R})+h.c.\,.\] (S42)Projecting onto patches near the three Van Hove points, the effective theory in momentum space reads

\[H_{\rm int}=-\tfrac{q}{4}\sum_{\alpha>\beta}(c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}c_{\beta}-c_{ \beta}^{\dagger}c_{\alpha})(c_{\beta}^{\dagger}c_{\alpha}-c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}c _{\beta})\,,\] (S43)

where summations over momentum \(|{\bf k}|<\Lambda_{c}\) within each patch are not written explicitly. Using a Hubbard-Stratonovich transformation, the quartic interaction can be decoupled with a real order parameter field \(\Delta_{\gamma}\) and the action takes the form

\[S=\int_{0}^{\beta}{\rm d}\tau\sum_{\alpha}c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}(\partial_{\tau}+ \epsilon_{\alpha})c_{\alpha}+\sum_{\alpha\beta\gamma}i\epsilon_{\alpha\beta \gamma}\Delta_{\gamma}c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}c_{\beta}+g^{-1}\sum_{\gamma}\Delta _{\gamma}^{2}\] (S44)

Imposing the static condition for the order parameter field \(\Delta_{\gamma}(\tau)=\Delta_{\gamma}\), the action in Matsubara frequency representation reads

\[S=-T\sum_{\omega_{n}}\sum_{\alpha}c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}(\omega_{n})G_{\alpha}( \omega_{n})c_{\alpha}(\omega_{n})+T\sum_{\alpha\beta\gamma}i\epsilon_{\alpha \beta\gamma}\Delta_{\gamma}c_{\alpha}^{\dagger}(\omega_{n})c_{\beta}(\omega_{ n})+\tfrac{T}{g}\sum_{\gamma}\Delta_{\gamma}^{2}\] (S45)

with the bare Green's function \(G_{\alpha}(\omega_{n})=1/(i\omega_{n}-\epsilon_{\alpha})\).

In the spinor basis \(\psi=(c_{1},c_{2},c_{3})^{\rm T}\), the action can be written as

\[S=-T\sum_{\omega_{n}}\psi^{\dagger}(\omega_{n}){\cal G}^{-1}(\omega_{n})\psi( \omega_{n})+\tfrac{T}{g}|\mathbf{\Delta}|^{2}\,.\] (S46)

Here \({\cal G}^{-1}=G^{-1}-\hat{\Sigma}\) is the full single particle Green function where \(G={\rm diag}(G_{1},G_{2},G_{3})\) and

\[\hat{\Sigma}=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}0&i\Delta_{3}&-i\Delta_{2}\\ -i\Delta_{3}&0&i\Delta_{1}\\ i\Delta_{2}&-i\Delta_{1}&0\end{array}\right)\,.\] (S47)

Integrating out the fermionic fields leads to mean field free energy

\[f=g^{-1}|\mathbf{\Delta}|^{2}-{\rm Tr}\ln\left(-{\cal G}^{-1}\right)\,.\] (S48)

In the vicinity of phase transition, the order parameter is small and we can perturbatively expand the free energy in \(\hat{\Sigma}\). With the constant part ignored, the expansion to fourth order reads

\[f=\tfrac{1}{g}|\mathbf{\Delta}|^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\,{\rm Tr}\left(G\hat{\Sigma} \right)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{4}\,{\rm Tr}\left(G\hat{\Sigma}\right)^{4}=f^{(2)}+f^{( 4)}\,.\] (S49)

The quadratic \(f^{(2)}\) term reads

\[f^{(2)}=\tfrac{|\mathbf{\Delta}|^{2}}{g}+{\rm Tr}(G_{1}G_{2})|\mathbf{\Delta}|^{2}= \chi^{(2)}|\mathbf{\Delta}|^{2}\,,\] (S50)where the coefficient

\[\chi^{(2)}(T)=\tfrac{1}{g}+\text{Tr}(G_{1}G_{2})=\tfrac{1}{2}\left( \tfrac{1}{g}-\chi^{\text{ph}}(T)\right)\] (S51)

is proportional to \(T-T_{c}\) near \(T_{c}\). The quartic term reads

\[\begin{split} f^{(4)}&=\tfrac{1}{2}\left(\text{ Tr}(G_{1}^{2}G_{2}^{2})(\Delta_{1}^{4}+\Delta_{2}^{4}+\Delta_{3}^{4})+\text{Tr}(G_{1}G_{2}G_{3 }^{2})(\Delta_{1}^{2}\Delta_{2}^{2}+\Delta_{2}^{2}\Delta_{3}^{2}+\Delta_{3}^{2 }\Delta_{1}^{2})\right)\\ &=\tfrac{1}{2}(Z_{1}|\mathbf{\Delta}|^{4}+2(Z_{2}-Z_{1})(\Delta_{1}^ {2}\Delta_{2}^{2}+\Delta_{2}^{2}\Delta_{3}^{2}+\Delta_{3}^{2}\Delta_{1}^{2})) \end{split}\] (S52)

with the coefficients given by \(Z_{1}=\text{Tr}(G_{1}^{2}G_{2}^{2})\) and \(Z_{2}=\text{Tr}(G_{1}^{2}G_{2}G_{3})\). Their diagrammatic representations are shown in Fig. S7. Direct calculation of \(Z_{1}\) and \(Z_{2}\) in the patch model shows that \(Z_{1}>Z_{2}>0\)[11]. Since \(Z_{1}-Z_{2}>0\), the second term in \(f^{(4)}\) promotes the formation of a 3\(\mathbf{Q}\) LCO with \(|\Delta_{\alpha}|=\Delta(\alpha=1,2,3)\). Additional to the \(Z_{4}\) translational symmetry breaking, this leaves a fourfold space of degenerate ground states. The system will choose one of the four states \(\Delta(1,1,1),\Delta(-1,1,1),\Delta(1,-1,1),\Delta(1,1,-1)\) spontaneously upon entering the symmetry broken phase.

On the other hand, we can obtain the renormalized quasiparticle bands in the LCO phase as shown in Fig. S4. Apparently the 3\(\mathbf{Q}\) LCO fully gaps out the Fermi surface and is hence energetically favored. Moreover, the 3\(\mathbf{Q}\) LCO breaks time reversal symmetry thus forming a Chern insulator with quantum anomalous hall response. Indeed, the highest valence band features a non-zero Chern number \(C=1\) for 2nn 3\(\mathbf{Q}\) LCO.

### Orbital magnetization of LCO

The loop current state breaks time-reversal symmetry, which induces an orbital magnetic moment of the \(n\)th band at momentum \(\mathbf{k}\)[12]

\[\mathbf{m}_{n}(\mathbf{k})=-i\frac{e}{2\hbar}\langle\nabla_{\mathbf{k}}u_{n \mathbf{k}}|\times(H_{\mathbf{k}}-\epsilon_{n\mathbf{k}})|\nabla_{\mathbf{k}} u_{n\mathbf{k}}\rangle,\] (S53)

where \(u_{n\mathbf{k}}\) is the periodic part of the Bloch wave function, \(H_{\mathbf{k}}\) is the Hamiltonian of the system and \(\epsilon_{n\mathbf{k}}\) is the energy dispersion of \(n\)th band. The orbital magnetic moment originates from a self-rotation of a wave packet around its center of mass and will couple to an external magnetic field. Besides the thermodynamic average of the orbital moment, the orbital magnetization \(\mathbf{M}\) has another contribution form the center-of-mass motion of wave packet. The total contribution is given by [12; 13]

\[\mathbf{M}=\sum_{n}\int_{\text{BZ}}\tfrac{d\mathbf{k}}{(2\pi)^{2}}\left[ \mathbf{m}_{n}(\mathbf{k})-\tfrac{e}{\hbar}\epsilon_{n\mathbf{k}}\Omega_{n}( \mathbf{k})\right]f(\epsilon_{n\mathbf{k}}).\] (S54)

